+H1, +H1.1, +H1.1.1, +H1.1.2, +H1.2, +H1.2.1, +H1.3, +H1.3.1, +H1.3.2, +H1.4, +H1.4.1, +H2, +H2.1, +H2.1.1, +H2.2, +H2.2.1, +H2.2.2, +H2.3, +H2.3.1, +H2.4, +H2.4.1, +H3, +H3.1, +H3.1.1, +H3.2, +H3.2.1, +H4, +H4.1, +H4.1.1, +H4.2, +H4.2.1. The tree was empty and there are no results yet, so I seeded it with 4 Lvl1 hypotheses on different directions. Each has Lvl2 mechanisms and Lvl3 predictions with numeric thresholds.

- **H1, epochs to target:** the main lever is cutting the number of epochs needed for 75%. Mechanisms:
  - an airbench96-style backbone with whitening and Dirac init;
  - Muon for the conv filters;
  - label smoothing 0.3 plus Lookahead/EMA;
  - alternating flip, which should matter more because test-time augmentation is banned.
- **H2, cost per epoch:**
  - keep data on the GPU and augment there, which matters a lot with only 4 CPU cores;
  - a vectorised optimizer, compiled step, fp16 with channels_last, and CUDA graphs;
  - progressive resizing 24→28→32 px;
  - hard-example filtering with a proxy model (low prior).
- **H3, fixed timed overhead:**
  - compile or autotune leaking out of build when a shape wasn't warmed up;
  - the cost of prepare() itself.
- **H4, accuracy margin:**
  - seed and time noise; a 3-seed mean below about 0.756 is fragile for the 40-seed judgment;
  - near 9 epochs, epoch count trades smoothly against margin.

Suggested order of experiments:
1. Run the unmodified template twice to measure noise (H4.1.1).
2. Test GPU-resident data on the template (H2.1.1), so data-pipeline gains aren't mixed up with architecture changes.
3. Port the airbench96-style net at 9 epochs with LS 0.3 (H1.1.1). The PR's reference to beat is about 8.1 s, but at 75.29% it is below our 0.753 bar.
4. Only then try Muon, progressive resizing and systems work, one change per run.

Every run needs a compliance check: eval timed at B = 1, 7, 512 and 1024, no real data used in build, and every training shape warmed up in build. Treat time differences under about 2× the measured noise as inconclusive, since the judging card is an A100 PCIe rather than the SXM4 used here.
